STEP 1 -- "Apply a named hardware IP ACL to the static LAG; matching traffic
           denied, non-matching permitted"                                PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Method: `access-group` is an INGRESS filter. Host->swi_b traffic ingresses port1.0.48 and
  only EGRESSES sa1, so the flow that meets an ingress ACL on the aggregator is the REPLY,
  swi_b -> host. The rule is therefore written in that direction. A control run with the
  rule written the other way is included below, because it is what distinguishes "the ACL
  does not work on a LAG" from "the ACL is not in that packet's ingress path".

  CONTROL -- rule written in the direction that never ingresses sa1:
x960-56YSQ(config)#access-list hardware t44992-lag
x960-56YSQ(config-ip-hw-acl)#deny ip 10.38.22.65/32 10.38.22.70/32
x960-56YSQ(config-if)#interface sa1
x960-56YSQ(config-if)#access-group t44992-lag
x960-56YSQ#show running-config interface sa1
  interface sa1
   switchport
   switchport mode access
   switchport access vlan 100
   access-group t44992-lag                 <-- the binding is accepted on the aggregator
x960-56YSQ#show platform classifier statistics utilization brief
  ACL                 1
  Total               3 / 1024 (0.29%)     <-- the rule IS programmed into hardware
x960-56YSQ#show access-list counters
  Packet Hits: 0                           <-- nothing ingressing sa1 matches it
host# ping -c 3 -I 10.38.22.65 10.38.22.70 -> 0% packet loss   (correctly NOT denied)

  MEASUREMENT -- rule written in the direction that does ingress sa1:
x960-56YSQ(config-ip-hw-acl)#no deny ip 10.38.22.65/32 10.38.22.70/32
x960-56YSQ(config-ip-hw-acl)#deny ip 10.38.22.70/32 10.38.22.65/32
x960-56YSQ#show access-list
  Hardware IP access list t44992-lag
      4 deny ip 10.38.22.70/32 10.38.22.65/32
host# ping -c 4 -I 10.38.22.65 10.38.22.70 -> 100% packet loss   <-- DENIED
host# ping -c 4 -I 10.38.22.66 10.38.22.70 ->   0% packet loss   <-- PERMITTED
x960-56YSQ#show access-list counters
  Packet Hits: 4                           <-- exactly the 4 echo replies that were dropped
  => STEP 1 PASS: a named hardware IP ACL bound to the aggregator denies the traffic its rule
     matches and leaves every other flow on the same path untouched. The hit counter matching
     the packet count, and the TCAM ACL entry appearing on binding, are both positive proof
     the rule is enforced in hardware rather than merely accepted by the parser.

STEP 2 -- "The ACL is effective on the member ports of the LAG"           PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Method: with the deny in place, each member is shut in turn so the remaining member is the
  only path to swi_b. If the ACL were bound to the aggregator in name only, one of these would
  forward.

  A) force the traffic onto port1.0.16
x960-56YSQ(config)#interface port1.0.2
x960-56YSQ(config-if)#shutdown
host# ping -c 3 -I 10.38.22.65 10.38.22.70 -> 100% packet loss   <-- still DENIED
host# ping -c 3 -I 10.38.22.66 10.38.22.70 ->   0% packet loss   <-- still PERMITTED
x960-56YSQ#show access-list counters  -> Packet Hits: 10         (was 4)

  B) force the traffic onto port1.0.2
x960-56YSQ(config-if)#interface port1.0.2
x960-56YSQ(config-if)#no shutdown
x960-56YSQ(config-if)#interface port1.0.16
x960-56YSQ(config-if)#shutdown
host# ping -c 3 -I 10.38.22.65 10.38.22.70 -> 100% packet loss   <-- still DENIED
host# ping -c 3 -I 10.38.22.66 10.38.22.70 ->   0% packet loss   <-- still PERMITTED
x960-56YSQ#show access-list counters  -> Packet Hits: 13         (was 10)

x960-56YSQ(config-if)#interface port1.0.16
x960-56YSQ(config-if)#no shutdown
x960-56YSQ#show static-channel-group
  Member:
   port1.0.2
   port1.0.16                              <-- both members restored
  => STEP 2 PASS: one ACL bound to `sa1` enforces on each member port independently. The deny
     held with either member carrying the traffic alone, and the hit counter advanced in both
     cases, so the rule is programmed against both members and not just one.

STEP 3 -- "Non-IP traffic is unaffected"                                  PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
host# ip neigh flush dev eth4
host# ping -c 3 -I 10.38.22.65 10.38.22.70       -> 100% packet loss (ICMP denied)
host# ip neigh show dev eth4
  10.38.22.70 lladdr 00:00:cd:42:30:29 REACHABLE  <-- ARP re-resolved THROUGH the LAG
  => STEP 3 PASS: with the ARP cache emptied, ARP request and reply crossed the LAG in both
     directions and the entry was relearned, while the IP traffic between the same two hosts
     stayed denied. An IP hardware ACL does not filter non-IP frames.

STEP 4 -- "Rules are evaluated top to bottom, first match wins"           PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
x960-56YSQ(config-ip-hw-acl)#1 permit ip 10.38.22.70/32 10.38.22.65/32
x960-56YSQ#show access-list
  Hardware IP access list t44992-lag
      1 permit ip 10.38.22.70/32 10.38.22.65/32
      4 deny ip 10.38.22.70/32 10.38.22.65/32   <-- identical match, lower precedence
host# ping -c 3 -I 10.38.22.65 10.38.22.70 -> 0% packet loss      <-- PERMITTED
x960-56YSQ(config-ip-hw-acl)#no 1
x960-56YSQ#show access-list
  Hardware IP access list t44992-lag
      4 deny ip 10.38.22.70/32 10.38.22.65/32
host# ping -c 3 -I 10.38.22.65 10.38.22.70 -> 100% packet loss    <-- DENIED again
  => STEP 4 PASS: two rules matching the identical flow with opposite actions resolve to the
     lower sequence number, and removing it restores the later rule. Evaluation is first-match,
     ordered by sequence, and removal by sequence number works.

STEP 5 -- "Specify source and destination as host and as network addresses"  PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  host form (STEP 1): 4 deny ip 10.38.22.70/32 10.38.22.65/32
     .65 -> 100% loss (denied)      .66 -> 0% loss (permitted)
  network form:
x960-56YSQ(config-ip-hw-acl)#no 4
x960-56YSQ(config-ip-hw-acl)#deny ip 10.38.22.70/32 10.38.22.64/27
x960-56YSQ#show access-list
      4 deny ip 10.38.22.70/32 10.38.22.64/27
host# ping -c 3 -I 10.38.22.65 10.38.22.70 -> 100% packet loss
host# ping -c 3 -I 10.38.22.66 10.38.22.70 -> 100% packet loss
  => STEP 5 PASS: the /32 form denied only the host it named while its neighbour passed; the
     /27 form denied both addresses inside the prefix. Masks select the intended range, and
     the two forms differ exactly where the mask says they should.

STEP 6 -- "With and without the vlan qualifier"                           PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
x960-56YSQ(config-ip-hw-acl)#no 4
x960-56YSQ(config-ip-hw-acl)#deny ip 10.38.22.70/32 10.38.22.64/27 vlan 100
x960-56YSQ#show access-list
      4 deny ip 10.38.22.70/32 10.38.22.64/27 vlan 100
host# ping -c 3 -I 10.38.22.65 10.38.22.70 -> 100% packet loss   <-- matching VLAN, DENIED
x960-56YSQ(config-ip-hw-acl)#no 4
x960-56YSQ(config-ip-hw-acl)#deny ip 10.38.22.70/32 10.38.22.64/27 vlan 1
x960-56YSQ#show access-list
      4 deny ip 10.38.22.70/32 10.38.22.64/27 vlan 1
host# ping -c 3 -I 10.38.22.65 10.38.22.70 -> 0% packet loss     <-- wrong VLAN, PERMITTED
  => STEP 6 PASS: the same address rule denied the traffic when qualified with the VLAN the
     traffic is actually in, and stopped denying it when qualified with a different VLAN. The
     qualifier narrows the match rather than being ignored.

STEP 7 -- "Repeat with the ACL applied via a service policy"              PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Method: an interface ACL is matched BEFORE any QoS policy on the same port, so the
  access-group is removed and proven gone first. Without that, the QoS path is never exercised.

  a) remove the access-group and prove the ACL path is dead
x960-56YSQ(config-if)#interface sa1
x960-56YSQ(config-if)#no access-group t44992-lag
x960-56YSQ#show running-config interface sa1
  interface sa1
   switchport
   switchport mode access
   switchport access vlan 100              <-- no access-group line
x960-56YSQ#show access-list
  Hardware IP access list t44992-lag
      4 deny ip 10.38.22.70/32 10.38.22.64/27   <-- the ACL still exists, just unbound
host# ping -c 3 -I 10.38.22.65 10.38.22.70 -> 0% packet loss    <-- proof the path is dead

  b) bind the same ACL through QoS
x960-56YSQ(config)#mls qos enable
  Traffic will stop while configuration is applied. Continue? (y/n): y
x960-56YSQ(config)#class-map t44992-cm
x960-56YSQ(config-cmap)#match access-group t44992-lag
x960-56YSQ(config)#policy-map t44992-pm
x960-56YSQ(config-pmap)#class t44992-cm
x960-56YSQ(config-if)#interface sa1
x960-56YSQ(config-if)#service-policy input t44992-pm
x960-56YSQ#show running-config interface sa1
  interface sa1
   service-policy input t44992-pm          <-- bound via QoS, and no access-group
x960-56YSQ#show platform classifier statistics utilization brief
  ACL                 0
  QoS                 3
  Total               5 / 1024 (0.49%)     <-- same single rule now costs 3 QoS entries, not 1

  c) with the rule narrowed back to a single host so .66 is a true control
x960-56YSQ#show access-list  ->  4 deny ip 10.38.22.70/32 10.38.22.65/32
host# ping -c 4 -I 10.38.22.65 10.38.22.70 -> 100% packet loss   <-- DENIED
host# ping -c 4 -I 10.38.22.66 10.38.22.70 ->   0% packet loss   <-- PERMITTED
x960-56YSQ#show access-list counters
  Packet Hits: 0                           <-- hit counters do NOT advance on the QoS path,
                                               although the deny is demonstrably enforced
  => STEP 7 PASS: the same named hardware ACL enforces on the aggregator through
     `service-policy input` as it does through `access-group`, with the access-group proven
     removed beforehand so the QoS path carried the result. Two platform behaviours are worth
     recording: the identical rule occupies 1 ingress classifier entry via access-group but 3
     via the service-policy path, and `show access-list counters` stays at zero hits on the
     QoS path, so that counter cannot be used to confirm a QoS-bound ACL is working.

